{
  "name": "swiss-typographic-ui-light",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Swiss/International Typographic design system - a grid-driven, print-derived 'objective information design' aesthetic built on a strict 12-column/baseline grid, flush-left grotesque sans-serif typography (Helvetica Neue/Inter), a black-and-white palette with a single disciplined red accent, hairline rules instead of shadows, and bold section numerals - for integrating this precise, rational Josef Muller-Brockmann-inspired style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, navigation, tables), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Swiss / International Typographic\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to any particular industry; the grid-driven \"objective information design\" aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: **The grid is the design.** Swiss (International Typographic) Style, born from Josef Müller-Brockmann, Armin Hofmann, and the Basel/Zurich schools of the 1950s-60s, treats layout as a mathematical, modular system — every element's position, size, and alignment is derived from an underlying column-and-baseline grid, never placed by eye. Typography carries the entire visual weight of the design; there is no illustration, no decoration, no texture standing in for content. This is design as **objective communication**, not personal expression — clarity and legibility are the aesthetic.\n\n**Vibe**: Precise, rational, confident, uncluttered, timeless, editorial-but-functional. It should feel like a well-run transit map, a Müller-Brockmann concert poster, or a meticulously art-directed annual report — never playful, never soft, never nostalgic.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"Nothing is centered by accident, and nothing is centered by default.\" Alignment is always **flush-left, ragged-right** for text (never centered, never fully justified, which creates uneven word-spacing \"rivers\"). Every block — headline, image, caption, rule — snaps to the same underlying grid columns as everything else on the page, so the whole layout reads as one coherent system even when individual sections look asymmetric.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Minimalist Monochrome**: Monochrome is strictly black/white, serif (Playfair Display), editorial-luxury, zero-radius but not explicitly grid-driven; Swiss is sans-serif (Helvetica/Grotesk-family), allows one disciplined accent red, and its entire identity is the visible/implied modular grid.\n- vs. **Neo-brutalism**: Neo-brutalism is maximal, loud, rotated, and chaotic-on-purpose; Swiss is minimal, quiet, and rigidly aligned-on-purpose. Both reject soft gradients and drop shadows, but for opposite reasons (raw energy vs. rational clarity).\n- vs. **Academia/Classical**: Academia is warm, ornamented, and serif-driven; Swiss is cool, unornamented, and sans-serif-driven. Where Academia frames content like a certificate, Swiss organizes content like a train timetable.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nA restrained, high-contrast palette — content and grid carry the design, color is a precision instrument, not decoration. Use these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names (e.g. CSS variables, Tailwind theme, shadcn tokens) rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n\n*   `background`: `#FFFFFF` (Pure White)\n*   `foreground`: `#111111` (Near-Black Ink) — used for the vast majority of type and rules\n*   `muted`: `#F2F2F2` (Grid Gray) — subtle section-background variation, never a primary surface\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#6E6E6E` (Slate Gray) — secondary text, captions, metadata\n*   `border`: `#111111` (hairline rules, 1px, solid — never soft gray, never dashed)\n*   `accent` (Swiss Red): `#E30613` — the single permitted color. Used sparingly: section numerals, one call-to-action per view, a rule underlining the single most important statement on a page.\n*   `accent-foreground`: `#FFFFFF`\n\n**Color Usage Rules**:\n1.  This is fundamentally a **two-tone (black/white) system with one disciplined red accent** — never introduce a second accent hue. If more categorical color is genuinely required (e.g. status indicators in a dashboard), derive it from value/weight variation (bold vs. regular, black vs. gray) before reaching for color.\n2.  Red is used for **emphasis and function**, never decoration — a single CTA button, a single pull-quote rule, a single \"01\" numeral. If everything is red, nothing is.\n3.  Photography, when used, is treated in stark black-and-white or a red/black duotone — never full, unfiltered color — to keep it visually consistent with the two-tone system.\n\n### Typography — The Primary Design Material\n*   **Family**: **'Helvetica Neue'**, or **'Neue Haas Grotesk'** where licensed; **'Inter'** as the most faithful widely-available web fallback (`font-family: \"Helvetica Neue\", \"Neue Haas Grotesk\", Inter, Arial, sans-serif`). A grotesque/neo-grotesque sans-serif is mandatory — no serif, no rounded, no display faces.\n*   **Weights**: Regular (400) for body and most labels, Bold (700) for headlines and emphasis. Nothing else — no light/thin weights (too fragile for the style's confident tone), no black/900 (too heavy — Swiss bold headlines are strong through *scale*, not extreme weight).\n*   **Alignment**: **Flush-left, ragged-right, always.** Never center text (headlines included) and never fully justify body copy.\n*   **Scale**: A precise modular scale tied to the baseline grid (see Grid System below) rather than arbitrary Tailwind steps — e.g. a base unit of 8px with type sizes at 14/16/20/24/32/48/64/96px. In Tailwind terms this maps roughly to `text-sm` → `text-base` → `text-xl` → `text-2xl` → `text-3xl` → `text-5xl` → `text-6xl` → `text-8xl`, but the underlying discipline is that every size is a deliberate grid-aligned step, not a free choice.\n*   **Section Numerals**: Large, bold, red numerals (`01`, `02`, `03`) label major sections — a direct Müller-Brockmann signature. Set in the same grotesque family, often at `text-4xl` to `text-6xl`, positioned in their own grid column to the left of the section's content.\n*   **Line Length & Leading**: Body copy constrained to 45-75 characters per line (`max-w-md` to `max-w-xl` depending on column width); `leading-snug` (1.3) for headlines, `leading-relaxed` (1.5-1.6) for body.\n\n### The Grid System — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC\nEverything in this style is subordinate to an explicit modular grid.\n*   **Column Grid**: A 12-column grid at desktop (`grid-cols-12`), collapsing to 6 at tablet and 4 at mobile. Gutter width is fixed and consistent (`gap-6` or a project-specific unit) — never varies within a layout.\n*   **Baseline Grid**: All vertical rhythm (line-heights, margins, section spacing) is a multiple of a single base unit (commonly 8px). Headlines, body text, and spacing all snap to this baseline so text across columns lines up horizontally even when column content differs in type.\n*   **Asymmetric, Not Centered**: Compositions are frequently asymmetric (e.g. content spanning columns 1-7 with a numeral or caption in columns 9-12) but always **grid-locked** — asymmetry here means \"off-center by design,\" never \"unaligned.\"\n*   **Visible Grid as Ornament**: Occasionally expose the grid itself as a design element — thin vertical hairlines (`border-l border-foreground/10`) between columns in a hero or section divider, echoing Müller-Brockmann's exposed-grid concert posters. This is the style's one permitted \"decorative\" gesture, and it must still be grid-accurate.\n*   **Implementation**: use CSS Grid (`grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr)`) or a Tailwind 12-col grid utility set consistently across the whole product; do not mix ad-hoc flexbox spacing into sections that are meant to read as part of the grid system.\n\n### Radius & Borders\n*   **Radius**: `0px`, everywhere, without exception. Rounded corners contradict the grid-and-ruler precision this style is built on.\n*   **Borders / Rules**: `1px solid` hairlines in `foreground` (black) for standard dividers; a `2px` or `4px` solid `accent` (red) rule for the single most important divider on a page (e.g. under a pull-quote, above a primary CTA section).\n*   **No dashed, dotted, or double borders** — a rule is a single, decisive line.\n\n### Shadows\n*   **None.** This is a flat, print-derived aesthetic — depth and hierarchy come entirely from grid position, type scale, weight, and the sparing use of red, never from elevation effects.\n\n## Component Styling Principles\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Shape**: Rectangular, `rounded-none`, generous but not oversized padding (`px-6 py-3` to `px-8 py-4`).\n*   **Primary**: `bg-foreground text-background` (solid black button, white text) — the \"default\" strong action.\n*   **Accent (rare, one per view)**: `bg-accent text-accent-foreground` (red) — reserved for the single most important action on a page.\n*   **Secondary/Outline**: `bg-transparent border border-foreground text-foreground`, inverting to solid `foreground` fill on hover.\n*   **Typography**: Uppercase or sentence-case bold label, `tracking-wide`, no icon decoration unless functionally necessary (an arrow for \"next,\" for example — nothing purely ornamental).\n*   **Interaction**: Instant, hard color invert on hover/active (`transition-colors duration-100`) — no scale, no shadow, no lift. Motion in this style is a state *change*, not a physical gesture.\n\n### Cards / Containers\n*   **Structure**: No card chrome by default — content sits directly on the grid, separated by whitespace and hairline rules rather than boxed containers. Where a bounded container is genuinely needed (e.g. a stat tile, a pricing block), use a plain `border border-foreground` rectangle with `rounded-none` and no shadow.\n*   **Separation**: Sections are divided by full-width or full-column-span hairline rules (`border-t border-foreground`), not by background-color shifts or cards.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Style**: `border-b-2 border-foreground` (bottom-border-only, underline style) or a full `border border-foreground` box — pick one and use it consistently. `rounded-none`. No inset shadow, no glow.\n*   **Focus**: Border weight increases (`border-b-2` → `border-b-4`) or shifts to `accent` red — a purely linear, structural focus indicator, not a soft ring.\n*   **Labels**: Small, bold, uppercase, positioned above the field on its own grid-aligned line — labels are typographic, not decorative chips.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: A flat, ungrounded horizontal bar — no background fill distinguishing it from the page, just a `border-b border-foreground` hairline beneath it.\n*   **Links**: Regular-weight text by default; the active/current page is marked by **bold weight** or a red underline rule, never a background pill.\n*   **Logo/Wordmark**: Set in the same grotesque family, bold, often paired with a grid-column-aligned tagline in regular weight beside it.\n\n### Tables & Data\nSwiss style is exceptionally well-suited to data-dense UI — this is its home turf.\n*   Flush-left column headers in bold, hairline `border-b` under the header row, no vertical rules between columns (let whitespace/gutters do that job), numerals right-aligned and tabular (`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`).\n\n## Layout Principles\n\n*   **Container Width**: A fixed max-width tied to the grid (`max-w-7xl` typical), with consistent gutter margins at every breakpoint.\n*   **Section Numerals**: Every major section opens with a bold red or black numeral (`01 —`, `02 —`) in its own grid column, functioning as both wayfinding and a graphic anchor.\n*   **Whitespace as Structure**: Generous but *calculated* whitespace — every margin and gap is a multiple of the baseline unit, never an arbitrary \"looks about right\" value.\n*   **Photography/Imagery Treatment**: Full-bleed within its grid columns (never inset with rounded corners or shadow), cropped to hard rectangular edges, treated in black-and-white or red/black duotone when the surrounding content is otherwise very text-heavy.\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Non-Genericness)\n*   **Exposed Grid Lines**: Thin vertical hairlines marking column boundaries in hero/section-divider areas — makes the underlying system visible rather than merely implied.\n*   **Oversized Section Numerals**: Bold red `01`, `02`, `03` numerals as primary navigational/graphic devices.\n*   **Flush-Left Everything**: No centered headlines, no centered body copy, no centered buttons within their column — deliberate asymmetry is a feature, not an oversight.\n*   **One Red Rule**: A single, decisive 2-4px red horizontal rule used exactly once per view to mark the most important statement or divider — its rarity is what gives it power.\n*   **Tabular Numerals**: Data and stats are set in tabular figures, right-aligned, for a precise, engineered feel.\n*   **No Illustration, No Iconography-as-Decoration**: Icons are used only where functionally necessary (e.g. a close button, a chevron), never as decorative flourishes — the grid and typography alone carry visual interest.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: Minimal, fast, and purely functional — `transition-colors duration-100` to `duration-150` for hover/focus state changes. No easing curves that suggest physicality (no bounce, no spring, no overshoot).\n*   **No hover-lift, no scale, no shadow growth** — this style does not simulate physical objects; state changes are flat, instant color/weight inversions.\n*   **Page transitions**, if present, should be a simple, quick cross-fade or a hard cut — never a slide, zoom, or parallax effect, which would undercut the print-derived flatness.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: Simple, geometric, single-weight line icons (2px stroke) or solid glyphs — never filled gradient icons, never rounded \"friendly\" icon sets.\n*   **Color**: `foreground` (black) by default; `accent` (red) only for functional alerts/emphasis.\n*   **Usage**: Sparse and purposeful — navigation chevrons, close/menu toggles, external-link indicators. Not used as decorative section dividers or card headers.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: The black-on-white (and white-on-black) base palette clears WCAG AAA (21:1) by construction; verify the red accent (`#E30613`) meets at least 4.5:1 against its background before using it for text (it generally requires a white background, not vice-versa for body-sized text).\n*   **Focus States**: Structural, not decorative — a border-weight increase or a red underline, always paired with a genuine `:focus-visible` state so keyboard users get unambiguous feedback despite the absence of soft focus rings.\n*   **Line Length**: The style's own discipline (45-75 characters per line) is itself a readability best practice — preserve it rather than letting body text stretch full-width on large screens.\n*   **Touch Targets**: Even with `rounded-none` buttons, maintain 44px minimum height/width for tap targets.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Grid Collapse**: 12 columns (desktop) → 6 columns (tablet) → 4 columns (mobile), with gutters shrinking proportionally but staying consistent within a breakpoint.\n*   **Typography Scaling**: Headlines step down through the same modular scale rather than arbitrary shrinking — e.g. `text-8xl` → `text-6xl` → `text-4xl` at successive breakpoints, always landing on a defined step.\n*   **Section Numerals**: Remain prominent even on mobile — they're key wayfinding, not decoration to cut first.\n*   **Navigation**: Collapses to a simple, flush-left stacked list in a full-screen overlay (still `rounded-none`, still hairline-divided) rather than a rounded dropdown card.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\nThis aesthetic is a skin, not a category restriction — apply it the same way regardless of domain:\n*   **Marketing / editorial sites**: full exposed-grid hero, numbered sections, a single red CTA rule.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: this style's data-table and tabular-numeral conventions map directly onto dense dashboards — flush-left headers, hairline row dividers, red reserved strictly for critical alerts/primary actions so it doesn't get diluted across a busy interface.\n*   **E-commerce / portfolios**: full-bleed black-and-white or duotone product/work photography, flush-left captions, price/spec data in tabular numerals.\n*   **Developer tools / docs**: the grid system and hairline-rule conventions suit dense reference documentation extremely well; code blocks stay `rounded-none` with a simple bordered container, monospace type substituting for the grotesque sans only inside code.\n*   **Mobile apps**: preserve flush-left alignment, hairline dividers, and the numeral-as-wayfinding pattern; red stays reserved for the single primary action per screen.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill swiss-typographic-ui-light   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
    "/ui-compiler SaaS dashboard --theme swiss-typographic-ui-light --include Dashboards,Forms",
